+### [Procedural Volume Rendering with Noise Perturbation](1370-procedural-volume-rendering-with-noise-perturbation.md)
+Procedural Volume Rendering with Noise Perturbation enhances coarse, low-resolution volumetric data by superimposing high-frequency details using procedural noise, creating wispy, cloud-like filaments suitable for real-time clouds, smoke, and fire. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Implicit Surface Primitives](1800-implicit-surface-primitives.md) or [Distance to Hypertextures](1555-distance-to-hypertextures.md) by avoiding the computational cost of increasing voxel resolution or recomputing geometric gradients; instead, it warps the sampling domain or modulates optical properties at render time. It differs from [Procedural Noise Functions](1799-procedural-noise-functions.md) by specifically targeting volume access coordinates or transfer function outputs rather than generating the base field itself.
 
